#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a314d43-645b-5c09-84cc-babb825962d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253749} {:answer/id #uuid "399960c6-8915-55cf-b499-430d9554395e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254174} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9f49c1-1e43-5f1b-aef8-2b18efec441e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255187} {:answer/id #uuid "c08c3b51-5261-57f3-8880-d02902e00fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255385} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeb2a0e-c3b9-59b0-8c51-5cca3ce4d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399960c6-8915-55cf-b499-430d9554395e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae42a556-6e64-58a1-9826-bc4a1d962a7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f3eb3e3-8dee-5051-9c40-8fe59ac84fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122319" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b2/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016a3368-f9d5-58aa-ab05-aad63edb1b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253680} {:answer/id #uuid "1e97ff28-e0bb-54b7-b8b3-9417ec6fd261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253929} {:answer/id #uuid "2371f7e7-c2e4-57e1-bd77-d71c072ed039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253988} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5e00e1-832f-5f55-83f9-b8c48153a1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255339} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf76d94-8303-5033-a055-32483b709927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e97ff28-e0bb-54b7-b8b3-9417ec6fd261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253929} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a51decc-a151-5408-9864-1176626e3665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bd8ece4-afaf-5640-9ad2-c2e8ef66ae37" :question/math-academy-id "q-122338" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/98/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e5f7fd-5f39-54d0-b24b-d6386ba4b120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{3-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254422} {:answer/id #uuid "7715943a-4b29-5731-aa1b-21b6a9f5bcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{1-x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254725} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7f8494-68d3-5ece-b55e-5a21d83663af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254759} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa6296-4ac5-5e74-907b-e485c987c549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255345} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa68c5c-78bd-533d-9a2b-0493c8ac2f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{2}^{3-x}+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa6296-4ac5-5e74-907b-e485c987c549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6837870e-c4ce-53d3-8d92-89b9b7c8217b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9efcc68b-3929-5bf9-ab56-4c19b9a57740" :question/math-academy-id "q-122341" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6d/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4364ff09-2922-52f1-bb5f-e67a3ba34a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254246} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2164e7-b48b-5848-83ea-558ab8d57139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254476} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc028c3-519a-5570-a621-cd76222813f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254492} {:answer/id #uuid "da22387e-a7bf-5ddb-9d10-96871c121ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255605} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a3bfe-c5a0-5f5f-b52c-5d5d734cceb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4364ff09-2922-52f1-bb5f-e67a3ba34a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254246} :answer-field/id #uuid "f893dcb4-30ee-50df-9369-932bd543c3cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec791f70-e6b1-5d28-9a5c-0e20d98d3307" :question/math-academy-id "q-122349" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/14/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39825938-ad5f-5161-82be-a58bd1817a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254173} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee5c0c6-750e-5668-bdc0-064e5c9b4f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254925} {:answer/id #uuid "a5170868-e27c-5ec2-bb54-083bfe57da02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{-2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255126} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd96b2d-0804-5239-be68-eadc7bae04bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255629} {:answer/id #uuid "e85d341b-3675-5698-9153-635f1bc811e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcd96b2d-0804-5239-be68-eadc7bae04bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255629} :answer-field/id #uuid "795c6c90-3b12-5b40-b826-7991e61fdee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac389cb5-50b0-5d9d-9387-c7be6e7524a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122352" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
